---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ProjectHeader from "../../components/ProjectHeader.astro";
import "../../styles/projects.css";
import ds from "../../data/ma/dataset.json";
import validation from "../../data/ma/validation.json";
import bsValidation from "../../data/ma/balance-sheet-validation.json";
import { CRITERIA, CRITERION_INFO, DEFAULT_FILTERS, DEFAULT_WEIGHTS } from "../../lib/ma/screen.ts";
import type { Dataset } from "../../lib/ma/screen.ts";
import { defaultOutput } from "../../lib/ma/ui.ts";

const data = ds as unknown as Dataset;
const n = Object.keys(data.companies).length;
const initial = defaultOutput(data, n);
const v = validation;
---
<Base title="M&A Screening Workbench | David Lindgreen" description="A preliminary screening of US-listed pharma and biotech companies against a hypothetical buyer brief, with adjustable weights, data-gap flags, ranking sensitivity and a screening memo. Screening only; first release.">
  <ProjectHeader current="projects" />
  <main id="main">
    <section class="wrap proj-hero">
      <h1>Strategic M&amp;A Screening Workbench</h1>
      <p class="proj-lede">Build a first shortlist of acquisition candidates for a hypothetical buyer, see which choices drive the ranking, and read what the screen does not know. It narrows a list. It does not say what a company is worth or that a deal can happen.</p>
      <p class="status">First release · in development · hypothetical brief · not independently reviewed</p>
      <div class="brief">
        <div><h2>Who it is for</h2><p>An analyst or corporate-development associate who needs a defensible first cut of a universe before deeper work.</p></div>
        <div><h2>The problem</h2><p>A long list is easy to rank by gut feel. A screen should state its criteria, show its gaps and show how fragile the order is.</p></div>
        <div><h2>What it supports</h2><p>Deciding which handful of companies deserve a proper look. It is not a valuation model, a fairness view or a recommendation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#brief">Brief</a><a href="#screen">Screen</a><a href="#memo">Memo</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#limits">Limits</a><a href="#downloads">Downloads</a></nav>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap" aria-label="Disclosures">
      <p class="callout"><strong>The buyer is hypothetical.</strong> "BuyerCo" below is invented for this exercise; it is not a real client, mandate or employer. Data is public (SEC XBRL for Forms 10-K). The author works at Sanofi; no Sanofi or other employer information is used. Built with AI assistance; the author is responsible for checking it. Educational only, not investment advice.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="band band-mint">
      <section class="wrap section" id="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-h">
        <div class="section-head"><h2 id="brief-h">The hypothetical brief</h2><p>BuyerCo, a fictional European specialty-pharma platform with a US commercial presence, wants a profitable, growing, US-listed commercial-stage company in the revenue range of $150m to $3bn that it could realistically absorb. Illustrative criteria, not a view of what any real buyer wants.</p></div>
        <div class="pane">
          <div class="tbl-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Criteria, scrolls horizontally on small screens" tabindex="0">
            <table class="tbl">
              <caption class="sr">Scored criteria and how each is scored</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Score from 0 to 100</th><th scope="col">Default weight</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{CRITERIA.map((c) => <tr><th scope="row">{CRITERION_INFO[c].label}</th><td>{CRITERION_INFO[c].measure}</td><td>{CRITERION_INFO[c].anchors}</td><td class="num">{DEFAULT_WEIGHTS[c]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="muted" style="margin-top:0.75rem">Hard filters come first: revenue range ({DEFAULT_FILTERS.minRevenue} to {DEFAULT_FILTERS.maxRevenue} $m), positive operating income, and complete core data. Scores use fixed anchors from the brief, not the other companies, so a company's score does not move when the universe changes.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="wrap section" id="screen" aria-labelledby="screen-h">
      <div class="section-head"><h2 id="screen-h">Screen {n} companies</h2><p>Change the weights and filters; the ranking, the data flags and the memo update. Weights are relative and are rescaled to total 100.</p></div>
      <div class="cols-2" style="align-items: start">
        <form id="screen-form" class="pane stack" onsubmit="return false" aria-label="Screening controls">
          <h3 class="sub-h">Weights</h3>
          <div class="weights">
            {CRITERIA.map((c) => (
              <div class="wrow">
                <label for={`w-${c}`}>{CRITERION_INFO[c].label}</label>
                <input id={`w-${c}`} name={`w-${c}`} type="range" min="0" max="50" step="5" value={DEFAULT_WEIGHTS[c]} />
                <output id={`wv-${c}`} for={`w-${c}`}>{DEFAULT_WEIGHTS[c]}</output>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <h3 class="sub-h">Filters</h3>
          <div class="fields">
            <div class="field"><label for="min-rev">Minimum revenue, $m</label><input id="min-rev" name="min-rev" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="any" value={DEFAULT_FILTERS.minRevenue} /></div>
            <div class="field"><label for="max-rev">Maximum revenue, $m</label><input id="max-rev" name="max-rev" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" step="any" value={DEFAULT_FILTERS.maxRevenue} /></div>
          </div>
          <label class="switch"><input type="checkbox" id="req-profit" name="req-profit" checked={DEFAULT_FILTERS.requireProfitable} /> <span>Require positive FY2025 operating income</span></label>
          <label class="switch"><input type="checkbox" id="incl-royalty" name="incl-royalty" checked /> <span>Count royalty-financing obligations as debt</span></label>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="policy">When a criterion has no data</label>
            <select id="policy" name="policy"><option value="renormalize">Drop it and rescale the other weights (flagged)</option><option value="zero">Score it 0 (pessimistic)</option></select>
          </div>
          <h3 class="sub-h">Your share prices (optional)</h3>
          <p class="sub-note">No prices are bundled: no licensed source was available. Paste prices from your own source, one line per company, as <code>TICKER price</code> (US dollars). Market capitalisation is your price times the share count on each company's 10-K cover page. Entering prices switches on the two market criteria. Nothing you paste leaves this page.</p>
          <div class="field"><label for="prices">Prices, one per line</label><textarea id="prices" name="prices" rows="5" spellcheck="false" placeholder="BCRX 7.25&#10;HALO 62.10"></textarea></div>
          <div class="field"><label for="price-date">Date of your prices</label><input id="price-date" name="price-date" type="text" placeholder="e.g. 2026-10-21" /></div>
          <p id="price-msg" class="muted" role="status"></p>
          <div><button type="button" id="screen-reset" class="btn-quiet">Reset to the brief</button></div>
          <p id="screen-error" class="error callout" role="alert" hidden></p>
        </form>
        <div class="pane prose" style="align-self: stretch">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">How to read the result</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Score</strong> is a weighted average of the criterion scores (0 to 100).</li>
            <li><strong>Rank range</strong> and <strong>in top 5</strong> come from 1,000 random re-weightings in which each weight is multiplied by a random factor of roughly 0.6 to 1.65. A narrow range and a high share mean the position does not depend on the exact weights.</li>
            <li><strong>Data flags</strong> say what is missing or needs a human look. Flags are not penalties.</li>
            <li>Companies are never ranked on numbers that were not found: missing data is flagged, then handled by the policy you choose.</li>
            <li><strong>Net debt</strong> includes royalty-financing obligations (sold future royalties) by default, because they are debt-like. Switch that off to see the effect: PTC Therapeutics carries $2.3bn of them.</li>
            <li><strong>Valuation and deal size</strong> appear only if you enter prices. They are your prices, not data this site vouches for.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pane" style="margin-top: 1.25rem">
        <p id="screen-summary" class="sub-note" role="status">{initial.summary}</p>
        <div id="screen-out" class="tbl-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Ranked shortlist, scrolls horizontally on small screens" tabindex="0" aria-live="polite"><Fragment set:html={initial.table} /></div>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:0.75rem">* From 1,000 perturbed weightings (seeded, reproducible). Legend for the bar: segments are criteria in the order scale, growth, profitability, cash, balance sheet, R&amp;D, valuation, deal size (the last two only when you enter prices).</p>
        <p class="legend legend-seg"><span class="s0">Scale</span><span class="s1">Growth</span><span class="s2">Profitability</span><span class="s3">Cash</span><span class="s4">Balance sheet</span><span class="s5">R&amp;D</span><span class="s6">Valuation</span><span class="s7">Deal size</span></p>
        <details class="data"><summary>Excluded by the filters</summary><div id="excluded-out"><Fragment set:html={initial.excluded} /></div></details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="band band-mint">
      <section class="wrap section" id="memo" aria-labelledby="memo-h">
        <div class="section-head"><h2 id="memo-h">Screening memo</h2><p>Generated from the numbers above with fixed sentence templates, so every statement can be traced. It always lists what was not assessed.</p></div>
        <div class="pane">
          <div id="memo-text" class="memo"><Fragment set:html={initial.memo.map((l) => `<p>${l.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;")}</p>`).join("")} /></div>
          <p><button type="button" id="memo-copy" class="btn-quiet">Copy memo</button></p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="wrap section" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="ev-h">
      <div class="section-head"><h2 id="ev-h">Evidence and validation</h2><p>Retrieved {ds.generated} (debt amounts read 2026-10-21) from the SEC's XBRL company-facts feed.</p></div>
      <div class="pane">
        <div class="checks">
          <div><h3>{v.totals.passed} / {v.totals.checks}</h3><p>automated checks pass: fiscal-year dates, one filing per company for the core figures, sign and size sanity.</p></div>
          <div><h3>{v.totals.filingTextPassed} / {v.totals.filingTextChecks}</h3><p>FY2025 figures found, as printed, in the company's Form 10-K text. This catches mis-scaled or wrong-period values.</p></div>
          <div><h3>{bsValidation.passed} / {bsValidation.total}</h3><p>checks on debt read from the balance sheets: each amount is found in the 10-K text, and companies recorded as having no borrowings show no debt line.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>What this does and does not show</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>21 unit tests cover the scoring, filters, royalty-debt and market-cap maths, missing-data handling and sensitivity logic, including hand-computed cases.</li>
            <li>The text check shows a figure is printed in the filing. It does not prove the right line was chosen; the tag used for every figure is in the download.</li>
            <li>Not checked against the filing text: {v.uncheckedAgainstFilingText.join(", ") || "none"} (10-K not downloaded). Sums of several tags (for example debt) are listed rather than searched.</li>
            <li>Debt: standard tags missed borrowings or debt-like items at several companies. For nine companies the amounts were read from the 10-K balance sheet and are re-checked by script: Ironwood's revolver and convertible notes ($585m), Ligand's convertible notes ($446m), PTC Therapeutics' convertible notes ($287m) and $2.3bn of sold royalties, BioCryst's $466m of royalty financing, Ardelyx's $26m deferred royalty obligation, and no borrowings at Supernus, Corcept, Vanda, Acadia, Exelixis, Neurocrine and Kiniksa. Operating and finance leases are not counted as debt.</li>
            <li>Share counts come from each 10-K cover page (SEC XBRL cover data; Ani Pharmaceuticals and Kiniksa read from the cover text). They exclude options, restricted units and convertible notes, so market capitalisation from them is approximate.</li>
            <li>Figures are as reported under US GAAP and include one-off items such as licence or milestone revenue. Margins of 40% or more and growth of 40% or more are flagged for that reason.</li>
            <li>Fiscal years: Exelixis uses a 52/53-week year; FY2025 ends 2 January 2026.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="band band-mint">
      <section class="wrap section" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-h">
        <div class="section-head"><h2 id="method-h">Method</h2></div>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Universe</h3>
          <p>{n} US-listed pharmaceutical and biotechnology companies filing Form 10-K, picked by the author from memory as a convenience sample. It is not a systematic screen of the market, so a good-looking result here says nothing about companies that are not in the list. Two tickers considered (CPRX, DVAX) had no matching SEC record on the retrieval date and were dropped.</p>
          <h3>Metrics</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Revenue growth: compound annual growth from FY2022 to FY2025 revenue.</li>
            <li>Operating margin: operating income / revenue. Cash generation: operating cash flow / revenue.</li>
            <li>Leverage: (debt + royalty-financing obligations - cash - short-term investments) / (operating income + depreciation and amortisation). Net cash scores 100; negative EBITDA with net debt scores 0.</li>
            <li>Valuation (optional): enterprise value = your price x cover-page shares + net debt, divided by EBITDA; 100 at 8x or less, 0 at 25x or more. Deal size: enterprise value, 100 between $1bn and $6bn. Both are the hypothetical buyer's illustrative preferences.</li>
            <li>R&amp;D reinvestment: R&amp;D expense / revenue, scored highest between 10% and 30% because the hypothetical buyer wants an innovative base without a pre-revenue profile.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Scoring and ranking</h3>
          <p>Each metric maps to 0 to 100 through the piecewise-linear anchors in the brief table. The composite is the weighted average; weights are relative. Ties share a rank. Sensitivity uses 1,000 seeded random re-weightings, plus the alternative policy for missing data.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="wrap section" id="limits" aria-labelledby="lim-h">
      <div class="section-head"><h2 id="lim-h">Limits and next steps</h2></div>
      <div class="cols-2">
        <div class="pane prose">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">Limits</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Screening only. No valuation model, premium, synergies, financing, accretion or dilution, LBO or antitrust analysis, and no view on whether anyone would sell.</li>
            <li>No market data is bundled. By default size is measured by revenue; valuation and deal size exist only if you paste prices, and then they are exactly as good as your prices (and approximate share counts).</li>
            <li>Royalty-financing obligations are counted as debt by default. They are repaid from royalty income and are often non-recourse, so this is conservative for BioCryst and PTC Therapeutics; the switch shows both views.</li>
            <li>No product concentration, patent expiry, pipeline, litigation or ownership information.</li>
            <li>The brief and the scoring anchors are the author's illustrative choices. Different anchors would give a different order.</li>
            <li>Small convenience sample; small evaluation of the data; not independently reviewed.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="pane prose">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">Next steps</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a licensed or redistributable price source so valuation does not depend on pasted prices.</li>
            <li>Use diluted share counts (options, restricted units, convertible notes) for market capitalisation.</li>
            <li>Add product concentration and patent-expiry data from the filings.</li>
            <li>Test the ranking against a second, independently written brief.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="band band-mint">
      <section class="wrap section" id="downloads" aria-labelledby="dl-h">
        <div class="section-head"><h2 id="dl-h">Downloads</h2><p>Every figure with its XBRL tag, filing accession and date.</p></div>
        <ul class="downloads">
          <li><a href="/downloads/ma-screening/ma-dataset.csv" download>Dataset with sources (csv)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/downloads/ma-screening/ma-dataset.json" download>Dataset with sources (json)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/downloads/ma-screening/ma-validation.json" download>Validation results (json)</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="wrap proj-foot"><p><a href="/projects/">All projects</a> · David Lindgreen · Frankfurt, Germany</p></footer>
  </main>

  <script>
    import ds from "../../data/ma/dataset.json";
    import { initScreen } from "../../lib/ma/ui.ts";
    initScreen(ds as never, Object.keys(ds.companies).length);
  </script>
</Base>
